1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,944
Every answer the twin gives has a price, and you can watch it.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:09,582
In the room, a neon sign shows what each call cost, to the millionth of a dollar.

3
00:00:09,632 --> 00:00:12,703
It reads the gateway's own reply, not a dashboard.

4
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:21,259
Every model call goes through the gateway, and every call carries four things: the provider, the model, the tokens, and the cost.

5
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:25,061
Local answers cost nothing.

6
00:00:25,111 --> 00:00:30,516
The paid fallback is Claude Haiku: ten cents per million tokens in, fifty cents out.

7
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,436
Why Haiku?

8
00:00:32,486 --> 00:00:33,750
In Hareet's words:

9
00:00:33,963 --> 00:00:39,360
"we chose haiku since our context base is small and manually evaluated haiku and sonnet."

10
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:45,821
Money is counted in whole micro-dollars, never a float, in the gateway's own database.

11
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,928
Every call is also traced in Langfuse, which runs inside the stack.

12
00:00:52,640 --> 00:01:02,390
And it's locked down: no route out, sign-up switched off, its own telemetry off, and no prompts or answers kept, unless sampling is switched on in development.

13
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:09,157
Grafana puts spend beside latency and errors: today, this month, and the fallback's own budget.

14
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:12,364
Why measure all of it?

15
00:01:12,414 --> 00:01:14,374
Because the meter is the control.

16
00:01:14,424 --> 00:01:18,677
Cloud billing alerts lag by hours; this one decides in real time.

17
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:25,629
A full evaluation run, a hundred and eighty-six cases on Haiku, cost about twenty-one cents.

18
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,932
A real-time cost meter. Built in the open, at hareet dot A I.
